    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d769">

                <pb facs="0911" n="767" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg767"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.91293/160: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-07-10T19:00:00+08:00">July 10, 1929—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 11—9:45 a.m.<anchor n="76" xml:id="d769-f1"
                        />]</dateline></opener>
                <p>556. My 542, July 5, 8 p.m.<anchor n="77" xml:id="d769-f2"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Following telegram was repeated by the Legation to Shanghai: <quote
                            rend="blockquote">
                            <p>“July 6, 5 p.m. Please communicate to the District Attorney<anchor
                                    n="78" xml:id="d769-f3"/> a copy of the note to me from the
                                Minister for Foreign Affairs dated June 18 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                    >17</hi>]<anchor n="79" xml:id="d769-f4"/> requesting the
                                deportation of Abend and request him in my behalf to advise me by
                                naval radio at his earliest convenience whether on the showing of
                                facts made in that note it is his opinion that prosecution (with a
                                view to deportation) could properly be undertaken in the United
                                States Court for China against Abend on the complaint of the Chinese
                                Government on the ground of the libelous or seditious character of
                                his journalistic correspondence.”</p>
                        </quote></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>In a reply dated July 9th Shanghai informs me Sellett is of the opinion
                        that deportation of American citizens from China is not possible under our
                        law but suggests that libel charge might be made against Abend if Chinese
                        officials concerned would appear to testify in the United States Court for
                        China.</item>
                    <pb facs="0912" n="768" xml:id="pg768"/>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>I propose, unless otherwise instructed, to reply to Wang’s note of June
                        17th to the effect that while it is not of course within my competence as an
                        administrative officer to take action in the matter, I have consulted the
                        American officials concerned; that they have now expressed to me the opinion
                        that deportation as requested by the Chinese Government is not possible
                        under the law but that should the Chinese authorities desire that the
                        Minister’s note of June 17th be made the basis of a libel action against
                        Abend in the United States Court for China, I should be happy to take the
                        matter up with a view to facilitating the proceedings to that end.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
